      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do u mean goblins?? i'm just asking about the server stuff. can u just explain the technical reason for the delay without the metaphors?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. To explain the technical reason for the delay, it's because DNS (Domain Name System) updates can take time to propagate across the internet. When you update the A records, you're essentially telling the DNS servers that a different IP address is associated with a particular domain name. However, these updates don't happen instantly.\n\nThe reason is that DNS servers are distributed across the globe, and they need time to update their caches and propagate the changes to other servers. This process involves a series of recursive queries, where a DNS server asks another server for the IP address associated with a domain name, and so on. It's a bit like a game of telephone, where the message (the IP address) is passed from one server to another, and it takes time for the message to reach everyone.\n\nAdditionally, some DNS servers may cache the old IP address for a while, which can also contribute to the delay. But don't worry, the updates will eventually propagate, and your client's website should be accessible from everyone's IP address.",
      "source": "target",
